The port rewrites upstream's Python in Elixir to run in a plugin on HAL-C2's MC. Each
file in mc/ names the upstream files it was ported from and what it changed. In short:

- The loop guard hears each turn's tool calls from the MC as they end, instead of from
  an agent CLI's hooks, and keeps its state in the plugin's process, not in files
  between hook processes.
- The judge is the user's text generation model in HAL-C2, run in the background as
  a job of the plugin's; the in-line (sampled) judge is not ported. The judge's verdict
  carries the redirect, which upstream wrote with a separate call.
- A redirect is delivered as a steer of the turn under way, with a notice in the
  thread that says why. The plugin never stops a turn itself: the last step is a
  notice that offers the person a stop.
- What counts as stuck is learned per project and provider from turns that ended
  green, kept as JSON in the plugin's data directory.
- The micro-skills' environment facts are learned from the turns the MC hears,
  per project, and stated ahead of a new conversation's first message instead
  of at a session-start hook; their state is JSON in the plugin's data
  directory, and a turn the feed partly missed is read back from its thread
  before it is learned from. Only upstream's generic vocabulary is ported
  (the plugin's reading), not the experiments' Python/git and Node
  vocabularies, and not its per-call directory tracking: the MC does not give
  a directory per call, so a `cd` inside one command still shapes its forms
  but a directory an earlier call left the shell in is not known. A person
  may drop a fact so it is never learned again, and restore it, which
  upstream had no way to do. The paid distiller that proposes facts beyond
  the environment's is not part of this port yet; `microSkills=on` does the
  same as `envfacts` until it lands, and its candidates will go through the
  lifecycle this port already has.
- Upstream's tool pruning is not part of this port yet.
